TOPICS.push({
  id: '5.6', unit: '5', ref: '5.3.1', title: 'Reflecting on research', short: 'Kolb, Gibbs, Rolfe; outcomes, methods, analysis, organisation; future practice',
  summary: 'Use models of reflection — Kolb’s experiential learning cycle, Gibbs’ reflective cycle and Rolfe’s “What? So what? Now what?” — to evaluate your research outcomes, methods, analysis and organisation, and to plan future development and improvements to reliability, validity and scope.',
  spec: [
    'Models of reflection: Kolb’s cycle of reflection; Gibbs’ reflective cycle; Rolfe’s reflective model',
    'Reflect on outcomes: did the research validate or reject the hypothesis? surprising or expected? new questions or ideas?',
    'Reflect on methods: suitable? data collection tool appropriate? challenges? could data collection be improved?',
    'Reflect on analysis: biases? could different techniques give different results?',
    'Reflect on organisation: planned effectively? timescales realistic and achieved? delays or obstacles?',
    'Use reflection to inform future practice: personal development opportunities; future research areas; actions to overcome development areas; improvements to increase reliability, validity or scope'
  ],
  learn: [
    { h: 'Kolb’s experiential learning cycle (1984)', html: `
[[d:kolb]]
<ol><li><b>Concrete experience</b> — doing something (carrying out the research).</li>
<li><b>Reflective observation</b> — reviewing what happened.</li>
<li><b>Abstract conceptualisation</b> — learning from it: drawing conclusions, linking to theory.</li>
<li><b>Active experimentation</b> — planning and trying out what you have learned.</li></ol>
<p>Strength: simple, emphasises learning by doing. Limitation: less guidance on feelings and detailed analysis.</p>` },
    { h: 'Gibbs’ reflective cycle (1988)', html: `
[[d:gibbs]]
<ol><li><b>Description</b> — what happened?</li>
<li><b>Feelings</b> — what were you thinking and feeling?</li>
<li><b>Evaluation</b> — what was good and bad?</li>
<li><b>Analysis</b> — what sense can you make of it? why?</li>
<li><b>Conclusion</b> — what else could you have done?</li>
<li><b>Action plan</b> — if it arose again, what would you do?</li></ol>
<p>Strength: structured and thorough, includes emotions. Limitation: can be time-consuming; description may dominate.</p>` },
    { h: 'Rolfe’s reflective model (2001)', html: `
[[d:rolfe]]
<ul><li><b>What?</b> — describe the situation: what happened, what was my role, what was the result?</li>
<li><b>So what?</b> — what does this tell me? What did I learn? What theory/knowledge applies?</li>
<li><b>Now what?</b> — what do I need to do next? What would I do differently? What are the consequences?</li></ul>
<p>Strength: quick, simple, easy to remember. Limitation: may be too brief for deep analysis.</p>` },
    { h: 'What to reflect on', html: `
<div class="tbl"><table><tr><th>Area</th><th>Questions</th></tr>
<tr><td><b>Outcomes</b></td><td>Did the research validate or reject the hypothesis? Were the results surprising or expected? Did it raise new questions or ideas?</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Methods</b></td><td>Were the methods suitable? Was the data collection tool appropriate? Any challenges implementing them? Could data collection have been improved?</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Analysis</b></td><td>Were there any biases in the analysis? Could different analysis techniques have produced different results?</td></tr>
<tr><td><b>Organisation</b></td><td>Was the research planned effectively? Were timescales realistic and achieved? Any unexpected delays or obstacles?</td></tr></table></div>
<p>Then use reflection to <b>inform future practice</b>: identify <b>personal development opportunities</b> (e.g. spreadsheet statistics skills, interviewing), <b>future research areas</b>, <b>actions to overcome development areas</b>, and <b>improvements</b> to increase reliability (repeat, larger sample), validity (piloted, unbiased tools, representative sample) or <b>scope</b> (wider population, longer timescale, extra methods).</p>
<div class="box tip"><b class="lbl">Good reflection is…</b><p>honest, specific and evidenced (“my 25 responses were all from one class, so…”) — not vague (“it went well”). Balance strengths and weaknesses and always end with concrete actions.</p></div>` }
  ],
  eqs: [],
  worked: [
    { q: 'Use Rolfe’s model to reflect on this: “My questionnaire on energy drink use got only 12 responses because I handed it out on the last day of term.”', s: ['What? I gave out 30 paper questionnaires on the last day of term and only 12 were returned, all from my own class.', 'So what? The small, opportunity sample means results are unreliable and not representative of all 16–18 year olds, so I cannot confidently accept or reject my hypothesis. I learned that timing and distribution method affect response rate.', 'Now what? Next time I would plan the survey earlier in my Gantt chart, use an online form shared via tutors across all year groups, aim for at least 60 responses using stratified sampling, and pilot it first.'], a: 'What / So what / Now what with specific actions.' }
  ],
  pitfalls: ['Describing what happened without evaluating it.', 'Being vague (“it went well”) instead of specific and evidenced.', 'Mixing up the stages of Kolb and Gibbs.', 'Not ending with actions for future practice.'],
  cards: [
    ['Kolb’s four stages?', 'Concrete experience → reflective observation → abstract conceptualisation → active experimentation.'],
    ['Gibbs’ six stages?', 'Description, feelings, evaluation, analysis, conclusion, action plan.'],
    ['Rolfe’s three questions?', 'What? So what? Now what?'],
    ['Four areas to reflect on (spec)?', 'Outcomes, methods, analysis, organisation.'],
    ['How can reflection inform future practice?', 'Personal development, future research areas, actions, improvements to reliability/validity/scope.'],
    ['Which model includes feelings?', 'Gibbs’ reflective cycle.'],
    ['How could you improve reliability?', 'Larger sample, repeats, consistent tools.'],
    ['How could you improve validity?', 'Pilot and unbiased tools, representative sample, triangulate methods.']
  ],
  quiz: [
    { q: '“What? So what? Now what?” is…', o: ['Rolfe’s reflective model', 'Kolb’s cycle', 'Gibbs’ cycle', 'HACCP'], x: 'Rolfe et al. 2001.' },
    { q: 'Which stage comes after “feelings” in Gibbs’ cycle?', o: ['Evaluation', 'Action plan', 'Description', 'Conclusion'], x: 'Description → feelings → evaluation → analysis → conclusion → action plan.' },
    { q: 'In Kolb’s cycle, “abstract conceptualisation” means…', o: ['learning from the experience / drawing conclusions', 'doing the task', 'trying it out again', 'describing feelings'], x: 'Stage 3.' },
    { q: '“Were timescales realistic?” reflects on…', o: ['organisation', 'outcomes', 'analysis', 'methods'], x: 'Spec category.' },
    { q: '“Could different analysis techniques have given different results?” reflects on…', o: ['analysis', 'organisation', 'outcomes', 'ethics'], x: 'Spec category.' },
    { q: 'Increasing the sample from 12 to 60 mainly improves…', o: ['reliability', 'the hypothesis', 'the title', 'the timeline'], x: 'And representativeness.' }
  ],
  exam: [
    { q: 'Compare Gibbs’ reflective cycle with Rolfe’s reflective model. [4]', m: 4, ms: ['Gibbs: six stages incl. feelings', 'Gibbs: structured, thorough but time-consuming', 'Rolfe: three questions — What? So what? Now what?', 'Rolfe: quick/simple but may be less in-depth; both end with action'] },
    { q: 'NEA-style task: Using a model of reflection, evaluate your own research project and identify improvements for future practice. [10]', m: 10, ms: ['names and applies a model correctly (Kolb/Gibbs/Rolfe)', 'outcomes: hypothesis accepted/rejected, expected/surprising', 'new questions raised', 'methods: suitability, tool effectiveness, challenges', 'improvements to data collection', 'analysis: bias, alternative techniques', 'organisation: planning, timescales, delays', 'personal development opportunities', 'future research areas', 'specific improvements to reliability, validity or scope'] }
  ],
  sims: ['reflect'], gens: []
});
